I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, and the spec makes that its entire authority. The graph definition hasn't been updated yet, and sending the input text to an external host would be an outbound data flow the node isn't granted. Once the graph includes the `http-client` grant, the world can be extended to match.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // The session identity is supplied by the host channel; it is never
        // fabricated here. Anything that would violate the output contract
        // (undelivered, or no session id) traps instead of returning.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("ResponseChannel did not confirm delivery");
        }
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("ResponseChannel returned no session id");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

